#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:22
msgid "Plotting lines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:24
msgid "Plotting lines is handled by the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:28
msgid "import pygmt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:36
msgid "Plot lines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:38
msgid ""
"Create a Cartesian figure using the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.basemap` method. "
"Pass lists containing two values to the ``x`` and ``y`` parameters of "
"the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` method. By default, a 0.25-point thick, black, "
"solid line is drawn between these two data points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:45
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(x=[1, 5], y=[5, 9])\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:58
msgid ""
"Additional line segments can be added by including more data points in the "
"lists passed to ``x`` and ``y``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:63
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(x=[1, 5, 8], y=[5, 9, 4])\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:76
msgid ""
"To plot multiple lines, :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` needs to be used for each "
"line separately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:81
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(x=[1, 5, 8], y=[5, 9, 4])\n"
"fig.plot(x=[2, 4, 9], y=[3, 7, 5])\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:97
msgid "Change line attributes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:99
msgid ""
"The line attributes can be set by the ``pen`` parameter which takes a string "
"argument with the optional values *width*,\\ *color*,\\ *style*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:104
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Set the pen width to \"5p\" (5 points), and use the default color "
"\"black\" and the\n"
"# default style \"solid\"\n"
"fig.plot(x=[1, 8], y=[3, 9], pen=\"5p\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:119
msgid ""
"The line color can be set and is added after the line width to the ``pen`` "
"parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:124
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Set the line color to \"red\", use the default style \"solid\"\n"
"fig.plot(x=[1, 8], y=[3, 9], pen=\"5p,red\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:138
msgid ""
"The line style can be set and is added after the line width or color to the "
"``pen`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:143
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Set the line style to \"..-\" (dot dot dash), use the default color "
"\"black\"\n"
"fig.plot(x=[1, 8], y=[3, 9], pen=\"5p,..-\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:157
msgid ""
"The line width, color, and style can all be set in the same ``pen`` "
"parameter. For a gallery example showing other ``pen`` settings, see :doc:`/"
"gallery/lines/linestyles`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:163
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Draw a 7-point thick, green line with style \"-.-\" (dash dot dash)\n"
"fig.plot(x=[1, 8], y=[3, 9], pen=\"7p,green,-.-\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:179
msgid "Connect points in different ways"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:181
msgid ""
"By default, consecutive points are connected directly. The ``straight_line`` "
"parameter can instead create right-angle connections. Use ``\"x\"`` to move "
"along the x-axis first or ``\"y\"`` to move along the y-axis first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/lines.rst:188
msgid ""
"x = [2, 8]\n"
"y = [2, 8]\n"
"\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X15c/10c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Connect the points directly\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, pen=\"2p,black\", label=\"Direct\")\n"
"# Move along the x-axis first, then the y-axis\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, straight_line=\"x\", pen=\"2p,red\", label=\"X first\")\n"
"# Move along the y-axis first, then the x-axis\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, straight_line=\"y\", pen=\"2p,blue\", label=\"Y first\")\n"
"# Mark the original points\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, style=\"c0.2c\", fill=\"white\", pen=\"0.5p,black\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.legend(position=\"BR\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

